Embedded Tor daemon - a licence carried by the build, not by this file:

  tor, installed by scripts/macos/build_macos_dmg.sh as Contents/Helpers/tor

    The binary is never committed. The script requires both POPOPX_TOR_URL and
    POPOPX_TOR_SHA256, verifies the digest of the downloaded asset before
    anything downloaded is unpacked or executed, and takes out of it the file
    named tor. The version a user receives is the version that build pinned, so
    no licence text for it is written here: measured 2026-11-23 this repository
    holds no tor binary or source (git ls-files | grep -cE '(^|/)tor$' = 0) and
    none was installed on the machine that wrote this notice (command -v tor
    returns nothing).

    What travels with the artifact is the asset's own notice. The script reads
    the LICENSE (or LICENSE.TXT, LICENCE, COPYING) out of the same verified
    archive and installs it at Contents/Resources/licences/tor/LICENSE before
    the bundle is signed. When the archive carries no licence file, an ad-hoc
    local build is warned and a Developer ID build stops: a BSD-style licence
    binds whoever redistributes to reproduce the copyright notice, the
    conditions and the disclaimer, and a DMG that shipped the binary without
    them would not. POPOPX_TOR_LICENSE names the licence text of the version
    being embedded when the asset itself has none. Both paths - licence
    installed, licence refused - were executed on 2026-11-23 against a synthetic
    archive with xcodebuild, codesign and hdiutil stubbed.

    The Tor Project publishes tor under a 3-clause BSD licence, with separate
    terms for parts of its own source tree. That is upstream's statement about
    upstream's bytes; the licence of the bytes in a given DMG is the file that
    came with the archive they were taken from. It is not retyped here, because
    a licence written from memory is not the licence of a binary.

    No pluggable-transport plugin (obfs4proxy, snowflake-proxy) is embedded: the
    script installs exactly one helper, so no notice is owed here for those, and
    PopopxTorKit refuses a bridge line whose transport needs one instead of
    starting a daemon that cannot use it.

